1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,486
In 2026, Japan declared a cybersecurity state of emergency.

2
00:00:05,486 --> 00:00:10,126
The reason was new: the attacks were powered by AI.

3
00:00:10,126 --> 00:00:15,733
Automated, adaptive, and faster than any human team could respond.

4
00:00:15,733 --> 00:00:22,529
Signature-based tools miss them, because AI never reuses the same pattern twice.

5
00:00:22,529 --> 00:00:27,243
So I built a tool to fight back. It's called Defender.

6
00:00:27,243 --> 00:00:32,106
An AI-powered attack detection and mitigation console.

7
00:00:32,106 --> 00:00:38,307
It runs completely offline, and nothing it scans ever leaves your machine.

8
00:00:38,307 --> 00:00:44,137
No cloud, no account, and no third-party service ever sees your data.

9
00:00:44,137 --> 00:00:50,263
And here's the key idea: to catch an AI attack, you use AI to defend.

10
00:00:50,263 --> 00:00:55,498
In the next twelve minutes, I'll show you everything it does.

11
00:00:56,798 --> 00:01:00,100
Everything starts on the dashboard.

12
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:05,261
One screen gives you your full security picture at a glance.

13
00:01:05,261 --> 00:01:09,157
In the center is your overall threat level.

14
00:01:09,157 --> 00:01:15,358
Right now this demo machine reads critical, with several serious findings.

15
00:01:15,358 --> 00:01:19,998
On the left, your most exposed ports, ranked by risk.

16
00:01:19,998 --> 00:01:25,679
Each one shows the application that opened it, and a clear verdict.

17
00:01:25,679 --> 00:01:31,509
On the right, the vulnerability board counts open issues by severity.

18
00:01:31,509 --> 00:01:37,041
Including the ones attackers are actively exploiting in the wild.

19
00:01:37,041 --> 00:01:42,276
Below that, every outbound connection, and its current state.

20
00:01:42,276 --> 00:01:46,990
The live alert feed streams detections as they happen.

21
00:01:46,990 --> 00:01:52,522
Every alert carries a severity, a score, the evidence, and a fix.

22
00:01:52,522 --> 00:01:57,683
All of it is powered by seven specialized detection modules.

23
00:01:57,683 --> 00:02:04,404
Log traffic, phishing, endpoints, prompt injection, ports, posture, and outbound.

24
00:02:04,404 --> 00:02:10,605
And the whole console is zero-dependency Python. No framework, no install.

25
00:02:10,605 --> 00:02:14,428
Let's see them run against a real machine.

26
00:02:15,728 --> 00:02:20,293
To analyze your own system, click scan this machine.

27
00:02:20,293 --> 00:02:24,933
Defender detects your operating system automatically.

28
00:02:24,933 --> 00:02:30,317
Windows, macOS, or Linux. It runs the right checks for each.

29
00:02:30,317 --> 00:02:36,369
Everything it does here is strictly read-only. It never changes a thing.

30
00:02:36,369 --> 00:02:42,347
It reads your listening ports, running processes, and live connections.

31
00:02:42,347 --> 00:02:47,731
Then your security posture: firewall, encryption, patch status.

32
00:02:47,731 --> 00:02:53,337
Watch the threat level. It resets, and reflects only this machine.

33
00:02:53,337 --> 00:02:59,167
No leftover demo data. Just the real state of your system, right now.

34
00:02:59,167 --> 00:03:04,253
Anything that needs admin to read is marked, never guessed.

35
00:03:04,253 --> 00:03:09,116
And the input consoles fill with exactly what was found.

36
00:03:09,116 --> 00:03:14,946
Your real hostname, your real ports, your real processes. Not a demo.

37
00:03:16,246 --> 00:03:19,399
Let's start with your open ports.

38
00:03:19,399 --> 00:03:24,559
Defender scores every listening socket by how exposed it is.

39
00:03:24,559 --> 00:03:30,537
Local-only ports are low risk. Ports open to the whole network are not.

40
00:03:30,537 --> 00:03:35,995
Each row shows the port, the service, and the program behind it.

41
00:03:35,995 --> 00:03:40,561
This one is a red flag: port four-four-four-four.

42
00:03:40,561 --> 00:03:44,755
The default port for Metasploit reverse shells.

43
00:03:44,755 --> 00:03:51,253
Opened by a program running from a temporary folder. A classic intrusion sign.

44
00:03:51,253 --> 00:03:57,232
Defender's verdict is clear: close. And it gives you the exact command.

45
00:03:57,232 --> 00:04:02,541
A reversible firewall rule, tailored to your operating system.

46
00:04:02,541 --> 00:04:07,924
Risky services like R-D-P and S-M-B are flagged the same way.

47
00:04:07,924 --> 00:04:14,794
On Windows it's a firewall rule. On Linux, a u-f-w command. On Mac, a p-f anchor.

48
00:04:14,794 --> 00:04:20,103
And every block is reversible, so you can undo it at any time.

49
00:04:20,103 --> 00:04:26,379
Low-risk, local-only ports are left alone, so you're not drowning in noise.

50
00:04:27,679 --> 00:04:31,501
Next, the vulnerability and posture audit.

51
00:04:31,501 --> 00:04:36,513
Defender audits your operating system's security controls.

52
00:04:36,513 --> 00:04:41,302
Firewall, disk encryption, automatic updates, and more.

53
00:04:41,302 --> 00:04:45,050
It flags how far behind your patches are.

54
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:49,838
Because automated exploit kits target known gaps first.

55
00:04:49,838 --> 00:04:56,188
Then it checks your installed software against a database of known exploits.

56
00:04:56,188 --> 00:05:03,133
Here, an outdated OpenSSH version matches a critical remote-code-execution flaw.

57
00:05:03,133 --> 00:05:07,922
Anything being exploited in the wild is marked clearly.

58
00:05:07,922 --> 00:05:12,264
And every finding comes with a full fix playbook.

59
00:05:12,264 --> 00:05:17,127
Why it matters, the steps, and the exact command to run.

60
00:05:17,127 --> 00:05:22,882
The CVE list is a starter set you can extend with your own feeds.

61
00:05:22,882 --> 00:05:28,043
Import a fuller database, and the matcher just gets smarter.

62
00:05:28,043 --> 00:05:34,318
Each finding is scored, so you always know what's urgent and what can wait.

63
00:05:35,618 --> 00:05:38,995
Now the outbound connection monitor.

64
00:05:38,995 --> 00:05:44,973
It watches which applications are connecting out, and exactly where to.

65
00:05:44,973 --> 00:05:50,505
Here it caught a program sending traffic to a crypto-mining pool.

66
00:05:50,505 --> 00:05:53,658
A textbook sign of cryptojacking.

67
00:05:53,658 --> 00:06:00,454
And this file, run from Downloads, is talking to a known command-and-control port.

68
00:06:00,454 --> 00:06:06,432
But here's the clever part. Defender learns what's normal for each app.

69
00:06:06,432 --> 00:06:12,782
After a learning window, any brand-new destination is flagged as an anomaly.

70
00:06:12,782 --> 00:06:18,463
That's the behavioral baseline. It catches what no signature could.

71
00:06:18,463 --> 00:06:24,590
Each destination is also checked against reputation and known-bad ranges.

72
00:06:24,590 --> 00:06:30,419
One click blocks the destination, and can stop the program behind it.

73
00:06:30,419 --> 00:06:37,067
And if the app is trusted, you mark the destination safe to refine the baseline.

74
00:06:38,367 --> 00:06:42,263
Four more modules handle input you feed in.

75
00:06:42,263 --> 00:06:46,011
The log analyzer reads web and auth logs.

76
00:06:46,011 --> 00:06:51,841
It catches credential stuffing, recon sweeps, and injection payloads.

77
00:06:51,841 --> 00:06:57,373
The content analyzer scores messages for AI-generated phishing.

78
00:06:57,373 --> 00:07:02,459
Urgency language, look-alike domains, and mismatched links.

79
00:07:02,459 --> 00:07:06,653
The endpoint module inspects running processes.

80
00:07:06,653 --> 00:07:12,408
Reverse shells, encoded commands, and ransomware-rate file activity.

81
00:07:12,408 --> 00:07:18,014
And the L-L-M defense module protects your own AI applications.

82
00:07:18,014 --> 00:07:24,439
It detects prompt injection, jailbreaks, and secrets leaking in model output.

83
00:07:24,439 --> 00:07:30,045
As AI gets woven into more apps, that attack surface only grows.

84
00:07:30,045 --> 00:07:34,090
Seven modules, one unified view of your risk.

85
00:07:35,390 --> 00:07:39,733
Detection is only half the job. Let's fix things.

86
00:07:39,733 --> 00:07:45,191
Every alert has a resolve block with concrete, reversible steps.

87
00:07:45,191 --> 00:07:49,682
The commands are tailored to your operating system.

88
00:07:49,682 --> 00:07:54,322
One click copies the exact command to your clipboard.

89
00:07:54,322 --> 00:08:00,300
But Defender never runs it for you. You review, you decide, you run it.

90
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:05,535
No automated changes you didn't approve. You stay in control.

91
00:08:06,835 --> 00:08:10,137
All of this feeds the AI advisor.

92
00:08:10,137 --> 00:08:15,372
A real scan can surface dozens of issues. Where do you start?

93
00:08:15,372 --> 00:08:20,309
The advisor ranks everything by impact divided by effort.

94
00:08:20,309 --> 00:08:24,875
So the highest-value fixes rise straight to the top.

95
00:08:24,875 --> 00:08:29,366
And you can just ask it anything about your system.

96
00:08:29,366 --> 00:08:34,824
It answers using only your live scan results. No hallucinations.

97
00:08:34,824 --> 00:08:38,572
No internet, and no cloud model required.

98
00:08:38,572 --> 00:08:44,476
If you run a local model with Ollama, answers get more conversational.

99
00:08:44,476 --> 00:08:50,751
Each answer is grounded in the exact findings behind it, so you can verify.

100
00:08:50,751 --> 00:08:56,655
If you run a local model with Ollama, answers get more conversational.

101
00:08:56,655 --> 00:09:00,849
But everything works without it. All on-device.

102
00:09:00,849 --> 00:09:06,679
Fix something, hit refresh, and anything resolved drops off the plan.

103
00:09:07,979 --> 00:09:10,091
So that's Defender.

104
00:09:10,091 --> 00:09:15,177
Seven modules, real machine scanning, and reversible fixes.

105
00:09:15,177 --> 00:09:19,371
A grounded AI advisor, and zero dependencies.

106
00:09:19,371 --> 00:09:24,755
To run it yourself, it's one command: python three, app dot py.

107
00:09:24,755 --> 00:09:30,138
It opens in your browser, and everything stays on your machine.

108
00:09:30,138 --> 00:09:37,380
If this was useful, like, subscribe, and share it with someone who cares about security.

109
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:39,567
Thanks for watching.

